  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 608 PM EDT MON JUL 27 2026\n\nThe models remain in good agreement through Friday. An anomalously \nstrong upper level high will meander west over the Four Corners \nregion, with ridging dominating across the Rockies and High Plains. \nMeanwhile, troughing will remain in place across the eastern third \nof the CONUS. By this weekend, deeper eastern Pacific energy moves \nonshore over the Pacific Northwest, pivoting a ridge axis towards \nthe central Plains and Upper Midwest/Great Lakes region. Further \neast, a short wave trough will move southeast from the Midwest \ninto the Ohio Valley, reinforcing troughing across the East. \nThere continues to be high confidence regarding the drier and \ncooler weather expected across eastern Kentucky from Wednesday \nthrough Friday. As we come more under the influence of the trough \nthis weekend and into early next week, a return of more diurnally- \ndriven convection is anticipated, although details remain lower \nconfidence at this point.\n\nDrier air will be advecting in on low level north to northwest \nflow across eastern Kentucky on Wednesday, with highs retreating\nto the lower 80s and dew points dropping into the lower 60s. \nSurface high pressure will continue to build in for Wednesday \nnight, with patchy valley fog and temperatures cooling off to the \nmid to upper 50s in sheltered valley locations, while broader \nvalleys and ridges stay closer to the 60 degree mark. Modest 500 \nmb height rises and short wave ridging will maintain mostly clear \nskies and dry weather across our area through Friday. Temperatures\nwill remain closer to normal for late July, with highs averaging \nin the low to mid 80s and lows in the low to mid 60s. \n\nThis weekend and into early next week, rain chances will creep \nback into our area, as low level moisture increases and \ntroughing returns across the Ohio and Tennessee valleys aloft. \nAgain, PoPs will tend to peak in the afternoon hours, but\nfortunately, LREF mean PWATs are only peaking in the 50th-75th \npercentile versus climatology during this time frame, keeping any\nhydro threats more suppressed. Temperatures will remain \nseasonable, with highs in the mid 80s each day and overnight lows \nin the mid 60s.",
